e here  and  we are able to induce these oligomers  with the presence of dopamine  and actually just recently  we've developed these native oligomers  with no inducer and we're pretty excited  to  start working with those and they're an  excellent control for the dopamine  induced oligomers as well  so  here's our initial data from the the  dopamine  oligomers and we have shown that  they do seem to be more neurotoxic than  the preformed fibrils  so looking at primary mouse cortical  neuron viability  and we're measuring this by  mitochondrial dehydrogenase mtt activity  basically if the neurons are healthy  their mitochondria are healthy they're  able to to  degrade basically this this yellow dye  into this this purple crystal and you  can read that out in an assay and what  you'll see here um on the the left so  um that's the percent  for to vehicle control so the vehicle  control here is is up at 100  and you'll see  several of the different types of  fibrils here  and they do  coronal viability by  about 25 to to a significant degree  um monomer on its own is is not  affecting the the viability of these  neurons  and then on the right here you see the  dopamine oligomers and they are  significantly more toxic so you know  less than 50 percent viability um  compared to the 75 ish viability from  the preformed fibrils so we have found  that they are indeed more toxic  and we are  currently investigating the native  oligomers  just to confirm that  the dopamine isn't having any effect  itself  so that was  it for alpha nucleon i also want to  talk about tau a little bit here so  tau functions to stabilize microtubules  so microtubules are  pretty negatively charged  tau has a high pi it's it's more  positively charged it stabilizes these  microtubules and  through  some mechanism and and many different  have been studied uh  when tau is not bound to  these microtubules and again we're  talking about a very flexible um  relatively unstructured protein in  solution i