e this this transmembrane protein  that is cleaved by  various enzymes to release this small  soluble peptide  that very quickly aggregates and forms  these beta amyloid  plaques which are present in  both alzheimer's and parkinson's  fibril deposits  and  this is normally in the synapses of  neurons this is occurring and um  so it's it's it's relevant whether this  is occurring before alzheimer or before  um  tower alpha nuclean and inducing that  but that's definitely a possibility but  i think at the end of the day it's going  to be a combination of factors so it's  important to look at  all of these different proteins and  their oligomers and fibrils  on their own  so we have recently generated these a  beta 1 to 42 so those are the amino  acids  fibrils from a synthetic peptide  so what you'll see here on the left is  a scs-page  western plot with the 6010 antibody that  will detect  any  a beta  and  looking at the gel here monomer you just  get this this nice clean  um  signal from monomer protein down there  at five kilo daltons it's four and a  half  um and then in the fibrils  you actually see um on the gel here um  this formation at the  in the well and at the very top of of  the gel here so indicating a very large  size um these fibrils are forming and on  the right you'll see sort of  three different types that we have  um in development and we're continuing  to characterize these and  are looking forward to testing these in  in models as well  finally we've also been able to generate  um a beta oligomers from that synthetic  peptide so again here on the left  sds page western blot with the 6010  antibody  you'll see on um so the molecular weight  ladders on the very left lane the second  lane over you'll see this sort of faint  smear um of 37 kilobones up  indicating that oligomers are actually  forming here and more obvious is the  formation of these dimers and trimers  around  15 kilo daltons on this gel here  and on the right you'll see  those nice 